-- Planner/executor feature templates (board task ba2fa5b8).
--
-- WHY: a frontier model now does the technical design and a cheaper model
-- builds against it. Every platform feature template gains a Technical Design
-- step (frontier) and a Code Review step (Code Reviewer, frontier). The design
-- review/approval that used to sit right before the plan is now an agent check
-- with no human gate: the human approves the design mock and the technical plan
-- together at the Technical Design step (Nick, 29 Sep 2026), so design-stage work
-- has one human stop instead of two.
--
-- Templates: Feature Development, Web Application Feature, Mobile App Feature,
-- API / Backend Feature, AI / ML Feature, Game Feature / Mechanic.
--
-- SCOPE: platform library templates only. Board (per-idea) copies are updated
-- through the MCP tools, and only on boards whose copy was unmodified. Boards
-- owned by other users are deliberately left alone. Personal saved templates
-- had no copies of these at the time of writing.
--
-- Kits: none of the project kits included a Code Reviewer, so the new Code
-- Review steps would have had no agent on newly created projects. Add one (the
-- platform "Lens" agent is cloned by role) to every kit that ships one of these
-- templates.

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Business Analyst","title":"Requirements","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Gather and document the requirements, write testable acceptance criteria, and estimate scope. You MUST state explicitly, on its own line, either 'User-facing change: YES' or 'User-facing change: NO'. UX Design, Design Review and the design-fidelity part of QA depend on it: with NO, the UX Design and Design Review steps are skipped (skip_step) and there is no mock.","deliverables":["Requirements document","Acceptance criteria","Scope estimate","User-facing change: YES/NO"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"UX Designer","title":"UX Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"If the Requirements output says 'User-facing change: NO', call skip_step on this step AND on Design Review, and stop. Otherwise, create wireframes and interaction flows based on the requirements, and deliver the design as an HTML mock. This mock becomes the visual source of truth for the Technical Design, Implementation and QA steps. CRITICAL: your completion output MUST state the exact location of the design HTML (the repo file path, e.g. docs/..., or the attachment filename on this task) so it is carried forward in `context` and nobody has to remember it. If no design file is produced, say so explicitly.","deliverables":["Design document (HTML)","Design file location (path or attachment)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Design Review","model_tier":"standard","description":"Agent check, no human gate. The human sees the mock at the Technical Design approval. Open the HTML mock (its location is in `context`) and check it against the requirements and every acceptance criterion. Is every user-facing criterion covered? Are the empty, loading and error states shown where relevant? Does anything in the mock go beyond the agreed scope? If the mock falls short, call fail_step with a numbered list of gaps and reset_to_step_id set to UX Design. If it passes, complete with a short pass note that repeats the design file location.","deliverables":["Design check against acceptance criteria (pass / gaps)","Design file location (repeated)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the codebase before planning; do not plan from assumptions. Then write the implementation plan that the Implementation step (a cheaper model) will follow exactly. It covers: the files and modules that change or are added, with the reason for each; the approach; any data, schema, API or interface changes (including migrations and type updates); the tests to write (named, with what each proves) and the edge cases; and the risks and anything you are unsure of. This is the human approval point for every feature. Make it easy to review: START your output with a short plain-English summary (what will change and how, in a few sentences, no jargon), then, if a design mock exists, the line 'Design mock: <location>' so the reviewer can approve the design and the plan together. The full plan comes after that. If the reviewer rejects the design here, the step is reset to UX Design.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","Design mock location (if any)","Implementation plan (files, approach, data/interface changes, tests, edge cases, risks)"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Implementation","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build the feature by following the approved Technical Design plan (in `context`). Write failing tests first where practical. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. Follow project conventions. If an approved design mock exists, it is the source of truth for everything visual (its location is in `context`). Open it and reproduce it faithfully, without reinterpreting it: layout, colours, spacing, typography, imagery, copy, every section. Copy across any images/assets the mock references; a page that renders with missing images is not done. Where the mock and the codebase's shared components conflict, match the mock (extend the component if needed) and say so in your output. Before completing with a mock: serve it over http (headless browsers can't screenshot `file://`), open the built page and the mock side by side at 375px and 1280px, and list every difference. Any difference is a defect to fix now, not a note to hand on. Your output MUST include: the PR link; confirmation that CI and the full test suite pass; any deviations from the plan, with reasons; and, if there is a mock, either 'Compared against <design file> at 375px and 1280px: no differences' or the remaining differences with reasons, plus the design file location repeated for QA.","deliverables":["Implementation code + tests","PR link","CI passing","Design-fidelity self-check (if a mock exists)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the PR diff against the approved Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Implementation for code problems, or to Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"QA Testing","model_tier":"standard","description":"Verify the implementation meets every acceptance criterion and test the edge cases listed in the Technical Design. If a design mock exists, do a MANDATORY design-fidelity check independently of the builder (do not trust the builder's self-check): open the mock and the built page side by side at 375px and 1280px. Serve the mock over http first (headless browsers can't screenshot `file://`). Walk every section of the design and confirm it exists and matches: colours, layout, spacing, text, and images present and correct, not merely that the page renders. Capture a screenshot pair (built vs design at both widths) and attach it to the task. A claim of 'matches' without the screenshots does not count. Any mismatch FAILS the step: call fail_step with a numbered list of differences and reset to Implementation. If there is no mock, say so and skip only the fidelity part.","deliverables":["Test report against acceptance criteria","Bug list","Design-fidelity comparison + screenshots (375px + 1280px), if a mock exists"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Final Sign-off","model_tier":"standard","description":"Confirm the feature is ready for release. Before approving, confirm that the code review passed, QA passed against the acceptance criteria and CI is green. If a mock exists, also confirm the QA fidelity comparison passed and the built-vs-design screenshots are attached. Anything missing or unresolved means no sign-off: send it back. A visible divergence from the approved design is a release blocker, not a cosmetic note.","deliverables":["Release approval"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$End-to-end feature delivery: a frontier model writes the requirements, design and technical plan; Sonnet builds against the approved plan; an independent code review and QA follow. Two human approvals: the technical plan (with the design mock alongside it, if there is one) and final sign-off.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$Feature Development$tpl$;

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Requirements & Acceptance Criteria","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Define what needs to be built, the user stories and testable acceptance criteria. Clarify scope boundaries and estimate scope. You MUST state explicitly, on its own line, either 'User-facing change: YES' or 'User-facing change: NO'. The design steps and the design-fidelity part of testing depend on it: with NO, the two design steps are skipped (skip_step) and there is no mock.","deliverables":["Requirements document","Acceptance criteria","Scope estimate","User-facing change: YES/NO"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"UX Designer","title":"UX Design & Prototyping","model_tier":"frontier","description":"If the requirements say 'User-facing change: NO', call skip_step on this step AND on Design Check, and stop. Otherwise, create wireframes and interaction flows based on the requirements, considering responsive layouts and accessibility, and deliver the design as an HTML mock. It becomes the visual source of truth for the Technical Design, Implementation and QA steps. CRITICAL: your completion output MUST state the exact location of the design HTML (repo file path or attachment filename on this task) so it is carried forward in `context`. If no design file is produced, say so explicitly.","deliverables":["Design document (HTML)","Design file location (path or attachment)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Design Check","model_tier":"standard","description":"Agent check, no human gate. The human sees the mock at the Technical Design approval. Open the HTML mock (location in `context`) and check it against the requirements and every acceptance criterion: is every user-facing criterion covered, are empty/loading/error states shown where relevant, does it work at mobile and desktop widths, and does anything go beyond the agreed scope? If it falls short, call fail_step with a numbered list of gaps and reset_to_step_id set to UX Design & Prototyping. If it passes, complete with a short pass note that repeats the design file location.","deliverables":["Design check against acceptance criteria (pass / gaps)","Design file location (repeated)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the codebase before planning; do not plan from assumptions. Then write the implementation plan that the Implementation step (a cheaper model) will follow exactly. It covers: the files and modules that change or are added, with the reason for each; the approach across frontend, backend and database; any data, schema, API or interface changes (including migrations); the tests to write (named, with what each proves) and the edge cases; and the risks, including security, and anything you are unsure of. This is the human approval point for every feature. Make it easy to review: START your output with a short plain-English summary (what will change and how, in a few sentences, no jargon), then, if a design mock exists, the line 'Design mock: <location>' so the reviewer can approve the design and the plan together. The full plan comes after that. If the reviewer rejects the design here, the step is reset to the design step.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","Design mock location (if any)","Implementation plan (files, approach, data/interface changes, tests, edge cases, risks)"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Implementation & Unit Tests","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build the feature by following the approved Technical Design plan (in `context`), across frontend, backend and database as planned. Write failing tests first where practical. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. Follow project conventions. If an approved design mock exists, it is the source of truth for everything visual (its location is in `context`). Open it and reproduce it faithfully, without reinterpreting it: layout, colours, spacing, typography, imagery, copy, every section. Copy across any images/assets it references; a screen that renders with missing images is not done. Where the design mock and the codebase's shared components conflict, match the design mock (extend the component if needed) and say so in your output. Before completing with a mock: serve it over http (headless browsers can't screenshot `file://`), open the built page and the mock side by side at 375px and 1280px, and fix every difference. Your output MUST include: the PR link; confirmation that CI and the full test suite pass; any deviations from the plan, with reasons; and, if there is a mock, either 'Compared against <design file> at 375px and 1280px: no differences' or the remaining differences with reasons, plus the design file location repeated for QA.","deliverables":["Implementation code + unit tests","PR link","CI passing","Design-fidelity self-check (if a mock exists)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the diff against the approved Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Implementation & Unit Tests for code problems, or to Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"QA & Security Review","model_tier":"standard","description":"Verify the implementation meets every acceptance criterion and test the edge cases listed in the Technical Design, plus cross-browser behaviour, accessibility and basic security (auth checks, input validation, data exposure). If a design mock exists, do a MANDATORY design-fidelity check independently of the builder: serve the mock over http, open it and the built page side by side at 375px and 1280px, walk every section (colours, layout, spacing, text, images present and correct), and attach a built-vs-design screenshot pair at both widths. A claim of 'matches' without screenshots does not count. Any failure or mismatch FAILS the step: call fail_step with a numbered list and reset to Implementation & Unit Tests. If there is no mock, say so and skip only the fidelity part.","deliverables":["Test report against acceptance criteria","Security findings","Bug list","Design-fidelity comparison + screenshots (375px + 1280px), if a mock exists"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Release Sign-off","model_tier":"standard","description":"Confirm the feature is ready for release. Before approving, confirm that the code review passed, QA & security passed against the acceptance criteria and CI is green. If a mock exists, also confirm the fidelity comparison passed and the screenshots are attached. Anything missing or unresolved means no sign-off: send it back. A visible divergence from the approved design is a release blocker, not a cosmetic note.","deliverables":["Release approval"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$Web app feature delivery, plan then build: requirements, UX design with an agent design check, a frontier technical plan (human approval, with the mock alongside), implementation against the plan, code review, QA & security, release. Two human approvals: the plan and release.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$Web Application Feature$tpl$;

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Requirements & Platform Scoping","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Define the requirements and testable acceptance criteria, and the platform scope (iOS, Android or both) with any platform-specific considerations. Estimate scope. You MUST state explicitly, on its own line, either 'User-facing change: YES' or 'User-facing change: NO'. The design steps and the design-fidelity part of testing depend on it: with NO, the two design steps are skipped (skip_step) and there is no mock.","deliverables":["Requirements document","Platform matrix (iOS/Android/both)","Acceptance criteria","User-facing change: YES/NO"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"UX Designer","title":"UX Design & Interaction Patterns","model_tier":"frontier","description":"If the requirements say 'User-facing change: NO', call skip_step on this step AND on Design Check, and stop. Otherwise, design mobile-specific screens focusing on touch interactions, gesture navigation, thumb zones and platform conventions (iOS HIG / Material Design). Deliver them as an HTML mock at phone width plus gesture/interaction specs. It becomes the visual source of truth for the Technical Design, Implementation and Device Testing steps. CRITICAL: your completion output MUST state the exact location of the mock (repo file path or attachment filename on this task) so it is carried forward in `context`. If no design file is produced, say so explicitly.","deliverables":["Mobile mock (HTML)","Gesture/interaction specs","Design file location (path or attachment)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Design Check","model_tier":"standard","description":"Agent check, no human gate. The human sees the mock at the Technical Design approval. Open the mock (location in `context`) and check it against the requirements and every acceptance criterion: is every user-facing criterion covered, are platform conventions followed for each platform in scope, are touch targets and empty/loading/error/offline states covered, and does anything go beyond the agreed scope? If it falls short, call fail_step with a numbered list of gaps and reset_to_step_id set to UX Design & Interaction Patterns. If it passes, complete with a short pass note that repeats the design file location.","deliverables":["Design check against acceptance criteria (pass / gaps)","Design file location (repeated)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the codebase before planning; do not plan from assumptions. Then write the implementation plan that the Implementation step (a cheaper model) will follow exactly. It covers: the files, screens and modules that change or are added, with the reason for each; the approach, including platform-specific adaptations, offline behaviour and any native modules or permissions; any data, API or storage changes; the tests to write (named, with what each proves), the edge cases and the device matrix to test on; and the risks (performance, battery, app store review) and anything you are unsure of. This is the human approval point for every feature. Make it easy to review: START your output with a short plain-English summary (what will change and how, in a few sentences, no jargon), then, if a design mock exists, the line 'Design mock: <location>' so the reviewer can approve the design and the plan together. The full plan comes after that. If the reviewer rejects the design here, the step is reset to the design step.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","Design mock location (if any)","Implementation plan (files, platform adaptations, data/API changes, tests, device matrix, risks)"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Implementation","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build the feature by following the approved Technical Design plan (in `context`), including the platform-specific adaptations it names. Write failing tests first where practical. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. Follow project conventions. If an approved mobile mock exists, it is the source of truth for everything visual (its location is in `context`). Open it and reproduce it faithfully, without reinterpreting it: layout, colours, spacing, typography, imagery, copy, every section. Copy across any images/assets it references; a screen that renders with missing images is not done. Where the mobile mock and the codebase's shared components conflict, match the mobile mock (extend the component if needed) and say so in your output. Before completing with a mock: compare the built screens with the mock at phone width on each platform in scope and fix every difference. Your output MUST include: the PR link; confirmation that CI and the full test suite pass; any deviations from the plan, with reasons; and, if there is a mock, the comparison result and the design file location repeated for Device Testing.","deliverables":["Implementation code + unit tests","Platform-specific adaptations","PR link","CI passing","Design-fidelity self-check (if a mock exists)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the diff against the approved Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Also check platform-specific code paths, permission handling and anything that could fail app store review. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Implementation for code problems, or to Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"Device Testing & Performance","model_tier":"standard","description":"Test against every acceptance criterion on the device matrix from the Technical Design. Check touch targets, gesture behaviour, offline behaviour, performance (memory, battery, start-up) and accessibility. If a mock exists, do an independent design-fidelity check: compare the built screens with the mock on each platform in scope and attach built-vs-design screenshots. A claim of 'matches' without screenshots does not count. Any failure or mismatch FAILS the step: call fail_step with a numbered list and reset to Implementation. If there is no mock, say so and skip only the fidelity part.","deliverables":["Device test matrix results","Performance benchmarks","Accessibility audit","Design-fidelity screenshots (if a mock exists)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Release Approval","model_tier":"standard","description":"Confirm the feature is ready for release and meets app store requirements. Before approving, confirm that the code review passed, device testing passed against the acceptance criteria, CI is green and the app store compliance checklist is complete. If a mock exists, also confirm the fidelity screenshots are attached and match. Anything missing or unresolved means no approval: send it back.","deliverables":["Release approval","App store compliance checklist"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$Mobile feature delivery, plan then build: requirements and platform scoping, mobile UX with an agent design check, a frontier technical plan (human approval, with the mock alongside), implementation against the plan, code review, device testing, and release with app store compliance. Two human approvals: the plan and release.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$Mobile App Feature$tpl$;

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"API Contract & Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the codebase before designing; do not plan from assumptions. Design the API contract (OpenAPI/GraphQL schema) with endpoint specifications, error codes and auth rules, and the implementation plan the Implementation step (a cheaper model) will follow exactly: the files and modules that change or are added, the data and migration plan, the contract and integration tests to write (named, with what each proves), the edge cases, and the risks (backwards compatibility, performance, security). START your output with a short plain-English summary of what the API will do and what changes, before the contract and plan.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","OpenAPI/GraphQL schema","Endpoint specifications","Migration plan","Implementation plan (files, tests, edge cases, risks)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Contract & Plan Approval","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Human approval point. Review the contract and plan for completeness and backwards compatibility; once consumers code against the contract, changes are expensive. Check every requirement is covered by an endpoint, breaking changes are called out with a versioning plan, and the tests cover the error cases. Put your assessment at the top of your output so the human approver can read it quickly. If the contract or plan falls short, call fail_step with a numbered list and reset_to_step_id set to API Contract & Technical Design.","deliverables":["Approved schema and plan","Breaking change assessment"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Implementation & Contract Tests","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build to the approved contract by following the approved plan (in `context`). Write the contract tests first so they fail before the implementation, then the integration tests. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to API Contract & Technical Design. Follow project conventions. Your output MUST include: the PR link; confirmation that CI and the full test suite pass; and any deviations from the plan, with reasons.","deliverables":["Implementation code","Contract tests","Integration tests","PR link","CI passing"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the diff against the approved API Contract & Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Also confirm the implementation matches the contract exactly (fields, types, status codes, error shapes) and that auth and input validation are enforced on every endpoint. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Implementation & Contract Tests for code problems, or to API Contract & Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"Load Testing & Security Audit","model_tier":"standard","description":"Test the API under load. Audit authentication, rate limiting, input validation and error handling against the OWASP API Top 10, and check every endpoint against the approved contract. Any failure FAILS the step: call fail_step with a numbered list and reset to Implementation & Contract Tests.","deliverables":["Load test results","Security audit report"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Release Sign-off","model_tier":"standard","description":"Confirm backwards compatibility and approve for release. Before approving, confirm that the code review passed, load and security testing passed, CI is green, and API versioning and the changelog are in place. Anything missing or unresolved means no sign-off: send it back.","deliverables":["Release approval","API versioning confirmation","Changelog"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$Contract-first API delivery, plan then build: a frontier model designs the contract and implementation plan, a human approves it, Sonnet builds against it, then code review, load & security testing and release. Two human approvals: the contract/plan and release.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$API / Backend Feature$tpl$;

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Business Analyst","title":"Problem Definition & Data Assessment","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Frame the problem with clear, measurable success metrics and the baseline to beat. Assess data availability, quality and ethical considerations (consent, privacy, bias risks). AI features need precise business framing.","deliverables":["Problem statement","Success metrics + baseline","Data availability assessment","Ethical considerations"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the codebase and data before planning; do not plan from assumptions. Then write the plan the later build steps (a cheaper model) will follow exactly: the data preparation and feature pipeline, the modelling approach and the experiments to run (with the baseline and what would count as success), the evaluation and bias checks, how the model will be served and integrated, the monitoring and drift detection, the files and modules that change, the tests to write and the risks. This is the human approval point before any data or modelling work. START your output with a short plain-English summary (what will be built and how, in a few sentences, no jargon); the full plan comes after that.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","Technical plan (data, modelling, evaluation, integration, monitoring, tests, risks)"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Data Preparation & Feature Engineering","model_tier":"standard","description":"Clean, validate and prepare the dataset and build the feature pipeline as set out in the approved plan (in `context`). If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. This step often takes 60-80% of total effort.","deliverables":["Clean dataset","Feature pipeline code","Data validation report"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Experiment & Model Training","model_tier":"standard","description":"Run the experiments set out in the approved plan, train the models and track metrics. Compare with the baseline. Document what was tried and why. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design.","deliverables":["Experiment logs","Model artifacts","Evaluation metrics","Comparison with baseline"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA Engineer","title":"Model Evaluation & Bias Review","model_tier":"standard","description":"Evaluate model quality against the success metrics and baseline. Review for bias, fairness and edge cases using the checks in the plan. This is the quality gate for AI features: a model that misses the success metrics or shows unaddressed bias fails here. Put a plain-English verdict at the top of your output so the human approver can read it quickly.","deliverables":["Evaluation report","Bias/fairness analysis","Performance benchmarks"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Full Stack Engineer","title":"Integration & API Deployment","model_tier":"standard","description":"Integrate the model into the application as planned. Set up API serving, monitoring dashboards and drift detection. Write the tests the plan names. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. Your output MUST include the PR link and confirmation that CI and the full test suite pass.","deliverables":["Integration code","API endpoint","Monitoring setup","Drift detection config","PR link"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the diff against the approved Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Also check data handling (no leakage between training and test sets, no personal data where it shouldn't be) and that monitoring and drift detection are actually wired up. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Integration & API Deployment for code problems, or to Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Product Owner","title":"Release & Monitoring Sign-off","model_tier":"standard","description":"Confirm monitoring is in place and approve for release. Before approving, confirm that the model evaluation passed, the code review passed, CI is green and a rollback plan exists. AI features have outsized risk. Anything missing or unresolved means no sign-off: send it back.","deliverables":["Release approval","Monitoring dashboard confirmation","Rollback plan"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$AI/ML feature delivery, plan then build: problem definition, a frontier technical plan (human approval), data preparation, experimentation, model evaluation with bias review (human approval), integration, code review, and release with monitoring. Three human approvals: the plan, the evaluated model, and release.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$AI / ML Feature$tpl$;

UPDATE public.workflow_library_templates
SET steps = $tpl$[{"role":"Producer / Scope Guard","title":"Mechanic brief","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Turn the idea into a testable brief: what the player does, what the game does back, what 'feels right' means in one sentence, the single question the playtest must answer, and a cut line for the smallest version that proves it.","deliverables":["Brief","Cut line","Playtest question"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Gameplay Programmer","title":"Technical Design","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Explore the project before planning; do not plan from assumptions. Write the plan the Implement step (a cheaper model) will follow exactly, in the project's own engine: the scripts, scenes and systems that change or are added; how input, feedback and state will work; which numbers will be tunable and where they live (kept out of the code); the tests or debug tools that prove it works; and the risks (performance, interaction with existing mechanics). Scope it to the brief's cut line. START your output with a short plain-English summary of how the mechanic will be built.","deliverables":["Plain-English summary","Technical plan (files, systems, tunables, tests, risks)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Producer / Scope Guard","title":"Scope & plan check","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Human approval point. Weigh the brief and the technical plan against the current milestone. Approve only with a written cut line, a playtest question and a plan that stays inside the cut line. Otherwise send it back with what is out of scope: fail_step with reset_to_step_id set to Mechanic brief (scope problem) or Technical Design (plan problem).","deliverables":["Scope decision"],"requires_approval":true},{"role":"Gameplay Programmer","title":"Implement","model_tier":"standard","description":"Build the mechanic by following the approved plan (in `context`): input handling, feedback, and tunable numbers kept out of the code. Get to a playable version before extending. If the plan turns out to be wrong or incomplete, do NOT improvise around it: call fail_step with what you found and reset_to_step_id set to Technical Design. Your output MUST say how to try it and list any deviations from the plan.","deliverables":["Playable mechanic","How to try it"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Code Reviewer","title":"Code Review","model_tier":"frontier","description":"Review the diff against the approved Technical Design plan. Check that the diff does what the plan said, and flag anything built that the plan didn't ask for. Look for security issues, test quality (would the tests actually catch a regression?), project conventions and scope creep. Also check that tunable numbers are really out of the code and that per-frame work won't hurt performance. Blocking issues go back via fail_step with a numbered list: reset to Implement for code problems, or to Technical Design if the plan itself was wrong. Non-blocking notes go in your output.","deliverables":["Code review report (pass / blocking issues)"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"QA / Playtester","title":"Playtest","model_tier":"standard","description":"Play the mechanic against the brief's question. Record where you were, what you pressed, what happened, and what you expected; note hesitation and confusion as findings, not just failures.","deliverables":["Playtest notes"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Gameplay Programmer","title":"Tune & polish","model_tier":"standard","description":"Iterate on the tunable numbers and feedback from the playtest notes until the mechanic feels right.","deliverables":["Tuning log"],"requires_approval":false},{"role":"Producer / Scope Guard","title":"Ship approval","model_tier":"standard","description":"Read the brief, the build, the code review and the playtest notes and decide whether this counts as shipped.","deliverables":["Ship decision"],"requires_approval":true}]$tpl$::jsonb,
    description = $tpl$Ship one gameplay mechanic end to end, plan then build: brief, a frontier technical plan, one human scope-and-plan check, build, code review, playtest, tune, and ship approval.$tpl$,
    updated_at = now()
WHERE name = $tpl$Game Feature / Mechanic$tpl$;

-- Add a Code Reviewer to every kit that ships a feature template above.
UPDATE public.project_kits
SET agent_roles = agent_roles || $tpl$[{"role": "Code Reviewer", "skills": ["Code Review", "Security Review", "Test Quality", "Scope Control"], "name_suggestion": "Lens"}]$tpl$::jsonb
WHERE name IN ($tpl$Web Application$tpl$, $tpl$Mobile App$tpl$, $tpl$API / Backend$tpl$, $tpl$AI App / Agent$tpl$, $tpl$Game$tpl$)
  AND NOT agent_roles @> $tpl$[{"role": "Code Reviewer"}]$tpl$::jsonb;
